    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate prioritizing high-ceiling upside against favorable matchups, headlined by **Corbin Carroll** (87.14) and **Josh Naylor** (73.91). **Corbin Carroll** is our clear top play, thriving against a generous 4.74 xERA matchup for Sugano, compounded by an elite park factor and a top-of-the-order boost. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** remains a must-start as he prepares to exploit the suspect arm of Walker Buehler; while the park factor is slightly negative, the SP skill differential provides a massive 19% bump to his efficiency, making his 73.91 score one of the safest bets on the board. **James Wood** also earns a strong recommendation at 68.90, as his leadoff spot and xwOBA profile suggest a high floor, even with the modest handicap for facing Bassitt.\n\nWe are aggressively exploiting platoon edges today, particularly with **Kyle Karros** and **Mickey Moniak**, who both benefit from a substantial 15% SP skill advantage and a favorable park bump against E-Rod. **Vinnie Pasquantino** remains a core anchor of the lineup, boasting a 65.10 score driven by a massive 14% dynamic platoon advantage against the right-handed Leahy. Conversely, our toughest bench decision involved **Austin Riley** (51.43). He fell just short of the cut because the current optimization favors the speed-weighted profiles of our MI/SS slots and the platoon-heavy upside of our outfielders. While **Austin Riley** provides solid power, his neutral park and negative SP skill matchup against Tolle left him sitting on the outside looking in.\n\nRisk management remains a primary focus, as we are navigating several \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Dalton Rushing**, **Brice Turang**, **Cole Young**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Seiya Suzuki**. While we have modeled these based on historical norms, users should monitor the confirmed cards, specifically for **Cole Young**, who remains a marginal start (49.63) but offers latent value if he can exploit the slower delivery of Walker Buehler to swipe a bag. Finally, monitor the 36% rain risk hovering over **Travis Bazzana** in Minnesota; if that weather holds, his solid 55.21 efficiency score could be jeopardized by a shortened contest or early exit.",
